; Sys.Platform - Windows, as kernel32 bindings rather than syscall numbers.
;
; Selected by the `.windows.ax` filename suffix when compiling for
; `--target=windows-x86_64` (the arch-specific `.windows-x86_64.ax` is
; tried first and does not exist: the bindings below are the same on
; every Windows architecture, which is the reason `Platform.darwin.ax`
; serves both Darwin targets).
;
; THIS IS THE OTHER KIND OF PLATFORM MODULE. The three POSIX modules are
; tables of integers - syscall numbers and flags - and `Sys.ax` makes
; every kernel call itself through `__syscallN`. Windows has no syscall
; ABI a program may use: the numbers are private to each build of the
; kernel and Microsoft documents none of them. So this module answers
; `usesSyscallAbi` 0 and DOES THE WORK ITSELF, over an `extern
; "kernel32"` block, in functions `Sys.ax` calls where it would
; otherwise have called `__syscallN`. `Sys.ax`'s header records that
; this is the one exception to "the platform module is a table", and
; why.
;
; WHAT IS BOUND TODAY: the console and process half - `WriteFile`,
; `ReadFile`, `GetStdHandle`, `ExitProcess`, `GetLastError` - which is
; what a hello world and the three runtime traps need. Files,
; directories, processes, the clock, sockets, entropy and signals are
; a later phase; until then a `Sys.ax` function that needs one of them
; reaches a `__syscallN` arm, which on this target the compiler lowers
; to a trap (`axiom: no syscall ABI on this target`, status 74) rather
; than to a `syscall` instruction the OS gives no meaning to. Loud, and
; on purpose.
;
; THE 81 NAMES BELOW EXIST because `Sys.ax` names every one of them
; and is checked against whichever platform module resolves. Every
; syscall NUMBER is 0 and is never handed to a kernel - `usesSyscallAbi`
; keeps the arms that would pass it unreached. Flags and errno values
; keep their Linux spellings on purpose: `eExist`, `eIsDir`, `eAgain`
; are what `IO.ax` and `Sys.ax` compare a failure against, so the
; error-mapping shim (`winErrnoOf`) answers in those terms and the
; comparisons above it stay portable.
;
; THE WORD-WIDTH RULE, which every shim here obeys and which
; `docs/ffi.md` states for externs in general: every binding is `i64`
; in and `i64` out. A Win32 function returning BOOL or DWORD writes
; only eax and leaves the upper half of rax UNDEFINED, so a 32-bit
; result is masked (`(& r 4294967295)`) before it is tested; a
; function returning a HANDLE or a pointer answers a full 64-bit
; value and is NOT masked - masking one would turn INVALID_HANDLE_VALUE
; (-1) into 4294967295, which compares unequal to -1 and reads as
; success. Which is which is written beside each binding.
;
; DESCRIPTORS. `Sys.ax` speaks in file descriptors; Windows in
; HANDLEs. Descriptors 0, 1 and 2 map to `GetStdHandle(-10 / -11 /
; -12)`; any other value is taken to BE a HANDLE. That is sound
; because a Windows HANDLE is a multiple of 4, so 1 and 2 can never be
; one, and 0 is the null handle, which no successful open answers.
;
; NOTE ON EFFECTS. The shims here perform I/O and say so; the POSIX
; modules carry the same three names as pure stubs behind
; `usesSyscallAbi` 1, which is what keeps the four modules declaring
; one set of public names (`check-stdlib-api.sh` requires it).

(pub :: sysRead Int)

(pub fn (sysRead) 0)

